Reason

To avoid repetition and accidental differences.

Example, bad

class Date { // BAD: repetitive
 int d;
 Month m;
 int y;
public:
 Date(int dd, Month mm, year yy)
 :d{dd}, m{mm}, y{yy}
 { if (!valid(d, m, y)) throw Bad_date{}; }

 Date(int dd, Month mm)
 :d{dd}, m{mm} y{current_year()}
 { if (!valid(d, m, y)) throw Bad_date{}; }
 // ...
};

The common action gets tedious to write and might accidentally not be common.

Example

class Date2 {
 int d;
 Month m;
 int y;
public:
 Date2(int dd, Month mm, year yy)
 :d{dd}, m{mm}, y{yy}
 { if (!valid(d, m, y)) throw Bad_date{}; }

 Date2(int dd, Month mm)
 :Date2{dd, mm, current_year()} {}
 // ...
};

See also
: If the “repeated action” is a simple initialization, consider 
a default member initializer
.

Enforcement

(Moderate) Look for similar constructor bodies.

