content:
Reason

catch
-clauses are evaluated in the order they appear and one clause can hide another.

Example, bad

void f()
{
 // ...
 try {
 // ...
 }
 catch (Base& b) { /* ... */ }
 catch (Derived& d) { /* ... */ }
 catch (...) { /* ... */ }
 catch (std::exception& e) { /* ... */ }
}

If 
Derived
is derived from 
Base
 the 
Derived
-handler will never be invoked.
The “catch everything” handler ensured that the 
std::exception
-handler will never be invoked.

Enforcement

Flag all “hiding handlers”.

